Mild self-limiting viral myalgia without significant fever or systemic prostration
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Diffuse symmetrical muscle ache, Afebrile state, Maintained motor strength

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-024
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, diffuse muscle aching and fatigue requiring assessment to exclude rhabdomyolysis or acute inflammatory myopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden severe, widespread body aches and profound fatigue. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate muscle strength, assess hydration status, and confidently rule out dangerous conditions like rhabdomyolysis or occult sepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My whole body aches deeply, like I ran a marathon I didn't train for.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a diffuse dull aching pain localized in proximal muscle groups (thighs, shoulders, back) developing over the past 48 hours. Reports symmetrical bilateral muscular discomfort and mild generalized physical fatigue. Notes a complete absence of core temperature elevation above 38C. Maintains baseline ability to perform activities of daily living despite the discomfort. Denies dark urine, focal weakness, or localized joint swelling.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, movements are slow and deliberate due to generalized ache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fatigue is limiting normal household duties.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/76 | HR: 82 | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, appears fatigued but in no acute cardiopulmonary distress.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination reveals normal passive and active joint range of motion. Diagnostic triad confirmed: diffuse symmetrical muscle ache, afebrile state, and maintained motor strength. Lack of focal muscle weakness or observable atrophy. Deep tendon reflexes are normal and symmetric. Complete sparing of the distal small joints. No localized muscle swelling or extreme tenderness to light touch.
Environmental Observations: Patient resting on the couch with a blanket, room temperature comfortable.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking paracetamol with transient benefit.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild self-limiting viral myalgia without significant fever or systemic prostration - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Rhabdomyolysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained motor strength, normal urine output and color, and absence of extreme, localized muscle swelling or crush injury.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of focal joint effusion, erythema, or severe pain restricting passive joint movement.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute viral onset, lack of prolonged morning stiffness (>45 mins), and age demographic (if applicable).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The rapid onset of profound, diffuse myalgia necessitated an urgent physical examination to definitively exclude severe musculoskeletal breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) or early systemic sepsis. Delaying assessment carried a high risk of overlooking signs of focal weakness or end-organ hypoperfusion. The home evaluation confirmed preserved gross motor function, an afebrile state, and normal hemodynamics, safely categorizing the presentation as a benign viral syndrome and preventing unnecessary hospital transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance and education regarding viral prodromes. Advice on strict scheduled dosing of NSAIDs (ibuprofen) alternating with paracetamol for pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform gentle stretching for 5 minutes immediately upon waking to increase blood flow to aching muscles, using the immediate relief of physical stiffness as a powerful reward to maintain mobility.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Use of a heating pad for local symptomatic relief on the lower back.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms fail to improve after 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden inability to move a limb, extreme weakness, dark cola-colored urine, or a high unremitting fever with confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; viral infections can be dynamic and unpredictable in the first 72 hours.
Clinical Course: The severe muscle ache typically peaks at 48-72 hours and resolves independently over 3 to 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if a specific joint becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you become so dizzy you cannot stand up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A mild viral infection has released inflammatory chemicals into your bloodstream, causing your muscles to ache symmetrically. Your body uses this response to fight the virus.
Actionable Steps: Stay well hydrated. Take ibuprofen with food to reduce muscle inflammation. Rest, but do not stay entirely bedbound; gentle movement helps clear the inflammatory chemicals.

